From: Kestrel OS port <local>
Subject: [PATCH] linux_exec: 64K-align the loaded ARM64 kernel image

Rationale: AllocateAnyPages guarantees only 4K alignment, but the
ARM64 kernel EFI stub requires 64K (TEXT_OFFSET) load alignment.
Boots fail nondeterministically with "FIRMWARE BUG: kernel image
not aligned on 64k boundary" (photographed on Lenovo 83N3).
Over-allocate 15 pages and align the base up; the existing section
bounds check still holds (max shift 60K < 64K extra).

---
--- a/linux.c
+++ b/linux.c
@@ -65,10 +65,16 @@
                 return log_error_status(err, "Cannot read sections: %m");          /* Do we need to ensure under 4gb address on x86? */+        /* The ARM64 kernel requires 64K (TEXT_OFFSET) load alignment, but AllocateAnyPages only+         * guarantees 4K. Without this, boot nondeterministically fails in the kernel EFI stub with+         * "FIRMWARE BUG: kernel image not aligned on 64k boundary" (photographed on Lenovo 83N3,+         * 2026-10-11). Over-allocate 15 pages and align the base up inside the allocation; the+         * section bounds check below still holds because the shift is at most 60K. */         _cleanup_pages_ Pages loaded_kernel_pages = xmalloc_pages(-                        AllocateAnyPages, EfiLoaderCode, EFI_SIZE_TO_PAGES(kernel_size_in_memory), 0);+                        AllocateAnyPages, EfiLoaderCode, EFI_SIZE_TO_PAGES(kernel_size_in_memory) + 15, 0); -        uint8_t* loaded_kernel = PHYSICAL_ADDRESS_TO_POINTER(loaded_kernel_pages.addr);+        uint8_t* loaded_kernel = (uint8_t*) ALIGN_TO(+                        (uintptr_t) PHYSICAL_ADDRESS_TO_POINTER(loaded_kernel_pages.addr), 65536);         FOREACH_ARRAY(h, headers, n_headers) {                 if (h->PointerToRelocations != 0)                         return log_error_status(EFI_LOAD_ERROR, "Inner kernel image contains sections with relocations, which we do not support.");
